\description{
Answers the quality-control question \emph{"how does the panel we are about to
publish differ from the one we already published?"}, value by value.

The two vintages are matched on country, year and variable. Every such
\strong{cell} that has a value in either vintage gets one \strong{status}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"Same"}: the published value is unchanged, within \code{tolerance}.
\item \code{"Revised"}: the published value changed by more than \code{tolerance}.
\item \code{"Dropped"}: the published value is missing from the latest vintage.
\item \code{"Added (backfilled)"}: a new value for a year the previous vintage already
covered.
\item \code{"Added (new period)"}: a new value for a year after the previous vintage's
last year.
}

Cells that are \code{"Same"}, \code{"Revised"} or \code{"Dropped"} are the \strong{published
cells}: the values the previous vintage put on the dashboard. Shares such as
\code{pct_dropped} are computed over them. The counts are then rolled up per
variable, per data source and per institutional family. Write the result to
an Excel workbook and a Word summary with \code{\link[=write_vintage_comparison]{write_vintage_comparison()}}.
}
\details{
\strong{What is compared.} Only numeric columns present in both vintages, other
than the two key columns, are compared cell by cell. Text columns (e.g.
\code{country_name}, \code{region}) are ignored. Variables, countries and years present
in only one vintage are listed in \code{structure} instead, so a renamed variable
appears there as one variable removed and another added.

\strong{Tolerance rule.} A published cell counts as \code{"Same"} when
\code{abs(latest - previous) <= tolerance * max(abs(previous), 1)}. For values of
magnitude 1 or more this is a \emph{relative} tolerance (0.1\% of the old value by
default); below 1 it becomes an \emph{absolute} one (a difference of at most
0.001 by default), so indicators on a 0-1 scale are not held to an
impossibly tight standard.

\strong{Percentage change.} \code{pct_change} is \verb{100 * (latest - previous) / |previous|}. It is \code{NA} when the previous value was 0, since the relative
change is then undefined; in \code{cells} those rows are sorted ahead of all other
revisions.

\strong{Limits.} A \code{"Revised"} status says only that the number moved. It does not
say why (a source revision, a rescaling, or a processing change all look the
same). New period is decided by the year alone: a value for a year after the
previous vintage's last year counts as \code{"Added (new period)"} even for a
country that is new to the panel.

\strong{Errors.} Stops if either vintage lacks \code{country_col} or \code{year_col}, or has
more than one row for the same country-year pair.
}
